# CafeRecomp

## Folder layout

Everything for this checkout is under the `CafeRecomp` folder:

- `build/bin/botw_recomp.exe`: game launcher, beside the four runtime DLLs.
- `game/base`, `game/update`, `game/dlc`: extracted game assets (moved, not duplicated).
- `src`: runtime, audio, input, and GX2/D3D11 renderer sources.
- `tools`: diagnostic and development tool sources; compiled tools are in `build/tools`.
- `recomp/generated`: generated PowerPC recompilation sources.
- `third_party`: DolRecomp, Cemu, aurora, gx2gl, and ModernGekko-Template checkouts.
- `build/logs`, `build/captures`, `build/dumps`, `build/traces`: retained diagnostics.

The September 28 folder consolidation does not fix or certify scene rendering;
the most recent game test still showed the yellow-screen rendering bug.
Existing game data, source changes, test binaries, and logs were preserved.

Experimental static recompilation project for the Wii U release of *The Legend
of Zelda: Breath of the Wild*. The project targets the North American v208
(title version `0x00D0`) update RPX and uses the locally extracted base game,
update, and DLC as runtime data. Game data is not copied into the executable.

## Current scope

- Espresso/PowerPC code is generated by the bundled DolRecomp checkout.
- A native Windows runtime provides sparse Wii U memory, coreinit-style imports,
  filesystem access, input, audio, and an experimental GX2/D3D11 renderer.
- The update title overlays the base title, and DLC is kept as a separate mount.
- Diagnostic block limits and logs make unsupported imports reproducible.

This is an engineering bring-up project, not a finished replacement for Cemu.
BOTW is substantially larger and uses more Wii U services than the existing
SM3DW proof of concept, so gameplay compatibility requires continued HLE and
renderer work.

## Verified milestone

September 26: a bounded Latte pixel-shader translator now generates native HLSL
instead of requiring a hand-written material for each complete program hash.
It supports 16 interpolated inputs, float/integer ALU operations, VLIW group
semantics and DOT4, structured forward if/else, compact uniform register/block
references, normalized 2D sampling with separate texture/sampler bindings, and
eight color outputs including burst exports. The native path preserves actual
clip-space geometry, MRT attachment identities and independent write/blend
state. Source render targets remain GPU-resident. Shader caches compare full
contents and use bounded LRU eviction; uniforms are refreshed for each draw.
The extended vertex input layout requires a D3D11 feature-level 11 device.

All 20 CTests pass. New GPU-readback checks cover simultaneous ALU writes,
integer bit preservation, dot products, both branch outcomes, input 15, MRT
write masks, texture/sampler slots 4/3, swizzles, feedback rejection and live
uniform updates. Captured scene pixel programs 179 and 191 compile and execute
on the GPU with controlled test inputs; this is not a captured-scene replay.
Four existing captured GX2 postprocess regressions still pass, including a
padding-only copy-program mutation that exercises the general translator
through real GX2 binding, fetch, vertex execution and rendering. Vertex RNDNE
now uses round-to-nearest-even independently of the host rounding mode.

September 27: scene PS translation now also handles single-arm JUMP/POP
branches, indexed uniform VFETCH via draw-current structured GPU buffers,
texture LOD bias and signed whole-texel XY offsets. GPU readback regressions
exercise both branch outcomes, changing indexed block contents, positive and
negative offsets, and malformed joins/half-texel offset rejection. Captured
programs 174, 175, 176, 183, 186, 187 and 188 from `render_scene_shader_0926`
compile and execute with controlled geometry/textures and their captured
indexed uniform blocks; this is not full-scene replay.

Vertex SAMPLE/SAMPLE_L/SAMPLE_LZ now support normalized, base-mip guest-backed
2D textures, including signed/HDR float samples. Vertex samplers survive
display-list recording and context changes. R8/RG8/RGBA8 UNORM render targets
can also be sampled by vertex shaders through a cached readback without
evicting GPU ownership. Tests verify filtering, component selection and cache
refresh after subsequent clears. Other formats/dimensions/mips still fail
closed. Float render targets now support cached, full-precision vertex readback
as well, with clear/draw serials invalidating the cache. Capture runs log bounded scene-draw
rejection stages; the pixel test's `--audit <capture-dir> <variant>` reports
the first rejected instruction offset without opening a window.

**Full scene/Link rendering is still incomplete.** The `scene_uniform_0927`
live test reached scene assets but its GPU readback remained uniformly yellow
(255,252,198). Logs identify remaining vertex-program, texture-format and
texture-view failures. Depth/stencil attachments, cube/array textures, complete mip chains,
arbitrary shader loops/relative addressing and other instructions remain
unsupported. Native R16/RG16/RGBA16 float, R32/RG32/RGBA32 float and packed
R11G11B10 targets now preserve HDR values across GPU passes instead of using
BGRA8 storage. GPU regressions sample/export values above one and below zero,
check missing-channel defaults, packed unsigned clamping, format changes and
BGRA upload/readback conversion. GX2 clear routing is tested separately.
Software BGRA fallbacks still cannot preserve HDR precision; MRT and typed
target support alone do not make the deferred renderer complete. Unsupported
programs fail closed. No game shader binaries are included in the source.

The subsequent `scene_hdr_0927` live test used the six half/full-float target
formats and reached scene draws at frame 8084, but the final captured frame
remained (255,252,198). Packed R11G11B10 and full-precision vertex-target
readback were added afterward and verified by GPU regressions, not by a second
full game run. All 20 tests pass in `hdr_final_ctest_0927.log`. Scene rendering
is not claimed fixed by these format changes.

GPU clears no longer redundantly fill the CPU mirror of every render target;
software consumers read it back on demand. Native Yaz0 back-references use
bounded non-overlapping bulk copies (or memset for distance one) instead of
per-byte copying. Regression coverage includes overlapping guest buffers,
distance-three repetition, output clipping and invalid references. These
changes do not establish instant startup or complete scene rendering.

Repeated AX RUN/same-address updates no longer discard an already-prefetched
sample. A stereo ramp regression checks every sample across three mixer
frames; this fixes one possible periodic click source, not all loading-time
audio starvation. Retained worker/audio imports now yield immediately, as
the main thread already did, instead of retrying a wait through the full slice.

Earlier rendering work (September 23, 2026): native GPU execution now covers
four captured post-intro passes: texture copy, four-tap reduction and the two
separable three-tap blur shaders. Their vertex ALU executes actual fetched
inputs, uniform registers and exports; semantic IDs are mapped through the
vertex descriptor to physical registers (R0 is reserved). The GPU retains
clip-space W and interpolates the exported parameters. Source render targets
stay on the GPU, with texture component swizzles, blend state and scissor
preserved. GX2 full-Z/half-Z clipping and rasterizer enable are tracked through
display lists and context switches; full Z is converted from [-W,+W] into
D3D's [0,W]. A captured blur pass at Z=-W exposed this missing conversion.
Known passes with unsupported vertex code fail closed rather than
falling through to the old fullscreen texture blitter.

Color surfaces are now keyed by backing image, not the scratch descriptor the
game repeatedly reuses. Display lists snapshot the surface metadata, and tests
verify independent images and GPU-only four-pass ping-pong. The hidden/muted
`effects_full_0923` run executes all four postprocessing passes at frame 2011;
the previously missing horizontal blur is present.

The vertex executor now handles structured forward branches, integer/scalar
ALU operations, partial varying exports, and indexed VFETCH reads of packed
uniform vectors. Uniform blocks use GPU-native little-endian words; treating
them as big-endian had turned values such as 1.0 into invalid shader arithmetic.
Six captured effect vertex shaders (25–30) execute with their captured inputs.
Five matching effect pixel materials now run on the GPU, including two/three-
texture combinations, squared color, and alpha erosion. Draw-local vertex reuse
avoids executing identical indexed vertices repeatedly, with instance IDs in
the cache key. All five material modes drew in the hidden/muted
`effects_extended_0923` live run at frame 1735, including the full 576-index
effect draw. A subsequent captured frame visibly contains the game's central
light flare and horizontal beam (`botw_effect_material_verified.png` in that
run's capture directory), rather than only black output.

All 18 CTests pass, including silent GPU pixel-readback and ALU regressions.
Optional captured-program tests also verify GX2 binding/fetch/draw, zero-instance
rejection, per-instance cache isolation (including recorded replay), and rejection
of unsupported vertex control flow. The executor's
malformed-program corpus and uniform-fetch boundary tests pass AddressSanitizer.
This is **not Link or full scene rendering**. The earlier `effects_full_0923`
run became a uniform pale frame after the intro. The later verified flare is
still an effect, not a rendered character or environment. General model
shaders/materials, depth, native HDR formats, complete sampler/mip support, and additional
scene pipeline work remain. Texture-sampling vertex instructions, arbitrary
loops, and other unsupported operations still fail closed.

September 24: effect passes now consume captured pixel-sampler registers,
including wrap/mirror/clamp/border addressing and point/bilinear filtering,
with independent samplers for each of their three textures. Constructors,
display-list value snapshots and context restoration are implemented/tested.
This path still samples decoded base mip levels only; unsupported half-border,
custom-border and bicubic modes are rejected. GPU readback tests distinguish
wrap versus clamp, point versus bilinear, border alpha and independent bindings.

Ordinary mutex waiters now leave the cooperative runnable set until the final
recursive unlock. A regression checks 100 scheduler turns without re-polling,
then acquisition/release after wake-up. Retained main-thread import waits also
yield after their first unsuccessful poll. Guarded native SafeString contains
calls match 2,024 original-code cases and execute in the live resource loader.
These changes do not yet establish full-speed gameplay or instant startup.

September 24: live samples exposed a DLC-directory stat in the AOC handler
before it checked the import name. Every later graphics/coreinit import paid
for disk metadata I/O. The prefix guard now precedes that probe; actual AOC
enumeration still checks the filesystem. The import regression failed before
the fix with 50,000 disk probes / 5,106 ms and passes afterward with zero probes
/ 34 ms for the same 50,000 coreinit calls. These are isolated call timings,
not a gameplay FPS measurement. A conservative symbol-page index also avoids
scanning imports for ordinary uncached game PCs (50,000 lookups: 29 ms before,
below the millisecond timer resolution afterward). Range/overlap/address-space
boundary tests preserve lookup semantics.

The `scene_aoc_gate_retry_0924` run reached 994 opened assets, including terrain,
then stopped at `03E27924`, an Espresso user quantization register write.
The project-local instruction fallback now implements UGQR0–7 reads/writes,
advances PC, and feeds the same quantization state used by paired-single
loads/stores. Tests exercise the exact generated faulting instruction, byte
unpacking, user access, and rejection of invalid instructions. No exception
is skipped. Draw captures now leave room for scene shaders instead of filling
their limit with changing particle counts.

Windows audio playback now has a separate device-feeding thread and a bounded
stereo jitter buffer. Guest AX callbacks remain on the emulation thread. Healthy
samples are unchanged; starvation and recovery use 5 ms ramps, and repeated
starvation grows the reserve from 64 to at most 192 ms. This adds latency during
loading and does not fabricate missing game audio. The device-independent test
checks prefill, stereo identity, ramps, overflow and reset. The visible/audible
`scene_audio_ugqr_0924` test reached its first capture about 22 seconds after
launch; subsequent logs show roughly 32,000 samples/sec with no dropped frames
and four initial underruns, rather than continuously increasing underruns.
These observations are not proof of pop-free audio, instant boot or 60 FPS.
Full scene/Link rendering remains unverified and the shader limitations above
still apply. All 18 CTest checks pass.

The longer `scene_audio_ugqr_0924` run passed the prior CPU fault and opened
1,655 assets before its window was closed normally. Captures now include real
scene draws (for example variants 179–194); the final captured screen was still
black, not verified gameplay. Two additional render-path corrections follow:
recognized postprocess passes accept host-rendered non-RGBA8 sources, and
unknown nonindexed shaders no longer stretch a stale texture over the screen.
The captured-program regression verifies exact GPU pixels across formats 1A,
19, 816 and 820, and that an unknown shader preserves the destination rather
than overwriting it. This does not add native HDR storage or general scene
pixel-shader execution by itself. The September 26 work adds a bounded general
pixel translator; further pipeline work remains necessary for full rendering.

Earlier September 23 check: the hidden/muted run passes Start and
renders the game's introductory text, including "Breath of the Wild". The
later intro submits new effect shaders (16-byte vertex streams, uniform
blocks, BC4/BC5 textures), which were unsupported at that point. The subsequent
work above adds bounded effect paths; this is still **not playable gameplay**.
The earlier September 20 run's latest frame also contains intro text, not an
entirely black image. These longer traces correct the earlier short-run status.

Startup now uses the already-built full-page guest read table, keeping checked
reads at partial/cross-page boundaries and preserving store/reservation logic.
The Windows message pump is limited to one poll per 4 ms instead of polling
on every graphics import and scheduler slice. Local same-machine launch-to-first
render measurements were 81.21 seconds before and 48.28 seconds after these
changes (`boot_0923` / `fastboot_0923` logs and corresponding captures). This is
not instant boot, a controlled benchmark, or a 60 FPS claim. All 12 CTests and
the captured layout/font shader integration test pass. Opt-in draw captures
now include additional vertex streams and vertex/pixel uniform blocks for
reproducing the unsupported scene shaders.

The subsequent stereo fix corrects `AXSetVoiceDeviceMix`: its third argument
is a device index, and its matrix contains six TV channels with four bus
records each. The old implementation read only the first gain, leaving the
right channel silent. The mixer now reads every main-bus gain and ramp, and
uses Cafe's L/R/SL/SR/C/LFE ordering for stereo downmix. The new silent
`botw_audio_mix` regression fails against the old DLL and passes against the
updated system DLL, covering distinct channels, centered mono, surrounds,
ramps, and invalid matrices. This does not implement aux/reverb buses or prove
crackle-free playback under heavy loading.

Historical bring-up milestones follow.

The v208 RPX generates 2,259 C translation chunks (about 2.49 GB of generated
source). The small `botw_recomp.exe` launcher loads `botw_runtime.dll` (the
generated code), `botw_system.dll`, `botw_graphics.dll`, and `botw_audio.dll`.
`build/bin` contains only that EXE and those four DLLs. Diagnostic executables
are kept in `build/tools` and are run through CTest. Game dumps remain external.

Bring-up fixes verified on September 10, 2026 include a contiguous title MEM2
heap, full reads of the 77,006,492-byte TitleBG pack, a functioning core
rendezvous barrier, and corrected synchronization-object sizes. A hidden
24,000,000-block run passes the former sound-initialization and heap-list
stalls and records GX2 display lists. Subsequent fixes implement cache-line
zeroing, semaphore counts/waits, and main-thread event waiting. Correct event
waiting lets archive loading finish before startup consumes its resources;
the game now records non-null shaders, textures, and indexed draws without
invalid guest memory accesses in the tested path. Cafe quantization-register
defaults fix the later `psq_st` alignment exception at `0x03C0669C`.
The Cafe overlay arena now has independent backing at `0xA0000000`, rather
than aliasing MEM2. The old alias let compressed reads at `0xA0408800`
overwrite the live GX2B texture record at `0x20408800`. A regression test
verifies that these exact addresses remain independent. Checked native Yaz0
decoding covers complete and chunked input, including reused stream objects;
generation preserves its same-chunk entry hooks.
The loaded effect archive's TEXR root and all 229 GX2B sibling headers now
match the original decoded archive. The loader progresses past the former
corrupted texture-list loop. Semaphore waiters are suspended until a signal,
and `OSSleepTicks` honors its 64-bit deadline instead of busy-looping. Tests
exercise two competing semaphore workers and a sleeping worker through the
actual cooperative scheduler, including one-token wakeups and delayed return.
AX audio frames are paced at 96 samples / 32 kHz (3 ms), with regression
coverage for split callbacks, early polls, and delayed-frame catch-up. A
52,000,000-dispatch run clears the resource-wait stage and reaches the title's
resource-table sorting code, with zero guest exceptions or unmapped reads and
writes (`build/logs/boot_blocking_waits_20260912.log`).
Later startup exceeded the old 32-thread host limit and rejected three service
threads. The host now reserves 128 records, with compile-time checks for TLS,
CRT, and audio-memory separation and regression coverage beyond 32 threads.
Runnable startup workers receive 32-dispatch turns; blocked workers do not
consume those turns. Controller-device polling is bounded independently of
the much faster guest dispatch loop.
SAVE file/directory opens and stat now resolve account-relative paths below
`save/80000001` (the runtime's local slot 1) or `save/common` (slot 255),
instead of incorrectly looking for `0/caption.sav` in game content. Read-only
fixtures test both accounts, content isolation, and traversal rejection.
The title's save-completion polling loop retains its original code and flags,
but gets a shorter main-thread quantum to let the save worker initialize data.
The September 13 trace identifies a job-queue lock at `0x37FD99C4` where a
worker releases and reacquires within its fixed time slice. Contended main
turns now use two dispatches against one per worker. An isolated regression
executes the actual generated `0x030DF674` queue path. Shared-memory stores
also invalidate other CPUs' tracked atomic reservations; same-line and
cross-line stores are tested. These changes pass the real-code contention test.
On September 13, the game reached indexed draws and scan-buffer swaps. The
presenter was rejecting drawn surfaces unless an explicit clear had occurred;
accepting drawn surfaces fixes that output gate. Nine CTest cases now pass,
including a draw-without-clear presentation regression that still rejects
untouched buffers. A visible run (`build/logs/boot_present_drawn_20260913.log`)
presents 1280x720 frames. Its first GPU readback, visually verified in
`build/logs/botw_first_render.png`, shows the BOTW title artwork and logo.
The follow-up renderer recognizes the position prefix of BOTW's actual compact
layout vertex shader: pane size/origin in c14, model-view rows c3..c5, and
projection rows c7..c10, including homogeneous division. The later title
variant shifts those uniforms by one vector and exports position from R6.
Both captured shader variants pass projection, missing-uniform, altered-program,
and bounded-pane GPU coverage checks. Native submission converts the CPU's strip
corner order into perimeter order, avoiding missing triangular regions.
The captured frame in
`build/logs/render_layout/first.png` verifies that the logo is no longer stretched
over the full screen. R8 font atlases now follow the indexed glyph path rather
than a fullscreen blit. The presentation regression also checks glyph pixels
inside/outside their quad; passing a captured layout shader to that test checks
its projected corners and rejects modified shaders/missing uniforms.

Layout sprites now use native GPU quad composition, with CPU rendering retained
as a fallback. High-frequency scheduler timing is opt-in instead of doing eight
`clock()` calls per scheduling turn. CPU-heavy boot uses the same 32-dispatch
main quantum as normal frames; both observed save-poll states and the contended
job queue still yield in 1–2 dispatches. The user has confirmed visible title/button
rendering and input-triggered sounds. An intro sequence and playable gameplay
have NOT been verified; menu rendering and startup performance remain bring-up
work.
The later-frame capture `build/logs/render_layout_variants/latest.png` verifies
the corrected title logo after the shader switch, not just on the first frame.
That older run took approximately 206 seconds to its first capture. Menu text
remains incomplete. It ended on a window-close request with
zero guest exceptions/unmapped accesses, two acquired audio voices, and 615
audible audio frames (`boot_layout_variants_20260913.log` and its stdout log).
The import dispatcher now caches both symbol hits and misses instead of scanning
all 921 symbols on every ordinary guest dispatch. In the September 13 comparison,
first-frame time fell from 184 seconds (`boot_indexed_ex2.log`, 15:09:17 launch /
15:12:21 capture) to 61 seconds (`boot_import_cache.log`, 15:14:07 launch /
15:15:08 capture). These are local measurements, not an instant-boot guarantee.
The subsequent corrected-render run (`boot_indexed_guard.log`) reached its first
frame in 64 seconds (15:18:07 to 15:19:11); its later-frame capture
`build/logs/render_indexed_guard/latest.png` confirms that the fullscreen glyph
sheet is gone. The title still has incorrect text/button placement, and this run
does not verify passage into an intro, menu, or gameplay. All ten CTests pass.

`GX2DrawIndexedEx2` now routes non-instanced UI draws through both immediate and
recorded rendering. Base vertex offsets apply to decoded indices rather than
the index-buffer pointer; 16/32-bit little/big-endian indices are supported.
Unsupported indexed shaders no longer substitute a fullscreen blit of whatever
texture remains bound. That fallback exposed the complete glyph atlas after a
three-index title draw. General 3D and instanced shader execution are still
incomplete; this does not establish intro or gameplay compatibility. Tests cover
both draw APIs, positive/negative base vertices, all four index formats,
display-list replay, empty instancing, and rejection of the glyph-atlas fallback.

September 14: GX2 context switches and embedded context restore lists now restore
the implemented shader, texture, uniform, render-target, viewport, and scissor
bindings. Host display lists are retained in sparse, dynamically sized storage
instead of evicting still-live lists after 256 recordings. The regression that
replays the oldest of 300 lists failed before this change and passes afterward.
Recording also no longer silently truncates after 256 commands. `GX2CopyDisplayList`
now submits outside recording and embeds commands inside recording, preserving
the copy when the original buffer is subsequently re-recorded. Tests cover both
copy modes, source replacement, a 300-command list, and context restoration.
All ten CTests and both captured layout-shader tests pass.

The retained-list run (`boot_retained_lists.log`) reached its first captured
frame in 51 seconds (00:55:30 launch / 00:56:21 capture). Its later readback,
`build/logs/render_retained_lists/latest.png`, shows the A icon beside Start
instead of at the center, with no missing-host-display-list errors observed in
that run. Font shading remains incomplete, and this is still title rendering,
not verified menu/intro/gameplay progression. These fixes do not implement the
remaining general 3D pixel/vertex shader pipeline.

Loading workers now keep the 32-dispatch startup budget after the first frame:
showing the title artwork does not mean background resource preparation is
finished. Contended-queue and event-wait scheduling guards remain in place.
For reproducible transition diagnostics, `BOTW_TEST_START_SAMPLE=180` injects
one GamePad A press for three input samples after rendering starts, and logs
new main-thread paths around it as `test-start:`. This is opt-in only; ordinary
Explorer launches never synthesize input. Clear the variable after testing.
The trace uses a bounded lookup and only observes ten samples before Start,
avoiding expensive whole-boot coverage collection.

Regression tests
cover these synchronization operations and the exact quantized byte store,
in addition to memory boundaries and complete large-file contents.

This milestone is not playable gameplay. Later startup still encounters
unimplemented virtual callbacks, partial Cafe services, and incomplete
Latte/GX2 shader/fetch translation. Use the finite-block diagnostics to extend
those systems without confusing a successful build with full compatibility.

## Build

September 20 bring-up: host keyboard/XInput now targets the virtual Pro Controller
by default, rather than simultaneously pressing VPAD and KPAD. The Start/Decide
animation has been traced through completion and the Start screen reaches its
closed state; that does not establish a working next scene. A regression runs
the actual generated animation tick. Native rendering now handles the verified
textureless constant-color fullscreen triangle, compact BC4 layout panes,
the captured layout material multiply/add shaders, and the font shader's
coverage threshold and material tint. Packed `GX2SetBlendControlReg` state is
captured by value in display lists. Tests cover transparency, font coverage,
blending, scissor, modified-shader rejection, and recorded blend-state replay.
The 12 CTests pass. General 3D/gameplay and stable 60 FPS remain unverified;
live audio underruns and incomplete scene composition still need investigation.
AX deadlines now retain cadence through small scheduler delays while rebasing
after long stalls. The host audio queue primes two buffers before playback;
delivery-rate/underrun logs distinguish guest starvation from device failures.

The subsequent hidden/muted checks implement `GX2CopySurface` for base-mip
single-sample storage: raw guest copies preserve bits across linear/tiled layouts
and array slices, while rendered base-layer surfaces copy their current GPU pixels.
Display lists capture descriptors by value and copy pixel contents when replayed.
Mip copies and GPU-to-guest CPU readback remain incomplete. Layout panes can
sample live host surfaces instead of stale guest bytes. Color-control, channel-mask,
and blend-constant setters now survive recorded replay and context switches.
Unchanged decoded textures retain their GPU content version to avoid redundant
uploads. Audio catches up delays below 60 ms, still rebasing after longer stalls.
Swap-status queries write counts and 64-bit timestamps, and `GX2DrawDone`
waits on a native GPU event query instead of unconditionally returning failure.
Bottom-cycle timing is explicitly marked unsupported, following the reference
backend; it is not a fabricated performance measurement. Renderer resets clear
blend state and preserve unique texture content versions across allocation reuse.
Hidden runs ignore physical controller/keyboard input and use only explicit test
input. The off-screen GamePad frame has verified readable text and correctly
positioned artwork; this is **not** a verified gameplay frame. The TV output
after Start remains black in the latest completed visual check, with no 3D draws
in the captured frame. Do not treat successful tests as game-boot completion.

Requirements: Visual Studio 2026 with Desktop C++, CMake, and a legally dumped
Wii U copy of BOTW matching the sibling directories already configured here.

```powershell
.\generate.ps1   # only needed when the RPX/compiler changes
.\build.ps1 -Configuration Release
.\verify.ps1 -RunTests
```

Generated game code stays in `recomp/generated`; native build products stay in
`build`. The generator verifies the v208 RPX SHA-256 before producing code.
The first Release build is large and can take substantial disk space and link
time; incremental host-runtime builds reuse the generated object library.

## Run

```powershell
.\build\bin\botw_recomp.exe --blocks 100000
.\build\bin\botw_recomp.exe
```

Use a finite block count for bring-up. An unlimited run opens the native render
window and continues until the guest exits or the process is closed.

Environment variables:

- `BOTW_LOG_PATH` redirects runtime diagnostics to a file. Without it, Windows
  launches log to `%LOCALAPPDATA%/BOTWRecomp/logs/botw_<pid>.log`, outside the
  five-file launcher directory.
- `BOTW_TRACE_PERF=1` enables high-frequency scheduler timing/progress logs.
  Normal launches keep coarse progress logs without the per-phase timing cost.
- `BOTW_PROFILE_DISPATCH=1` samples main-dispatch duration during boot and logs
  the 24 largest sampled totals at the first rendered frame (or normal exit).
  Sampling is irregular to avoid matching the scheduler quantum; this is not
  a complete worker-thread or instruction-level profile.
- `BOTW_TRACE_THREADS=1` records registers, waits, and guest stack chains when
  a bounded run finishes, for diagnosing startup dependencies.
- `BOTW_STOP_AT_PC=0x02E3D614` ends a diagnostic run when the main scheduler
  observes that guest program counter; combine with thread tracing.
  `BOTW_STOP_PC_HITS=4096` waits for repeated observations before stopping.
- `BOTW_SNAPSHOT_AT_PC` takes one live thread snapshot after 4,096 observations
  without stopping the game; enable `BOTW_TRACE_THREADS` too. Optional
  `BOTW_SNAPSHOT_AFTER_BLOCKS` ignores earlier occurrences of that address.
- `BOTW_HEADLESS=1` hides the window while retaining the native renderer for
  bounded boot diagnostics.
- `BOTW_MUTE=1` disables host audio output without stopping guest AX callbacks.
- `BOTW_MONITOR=secondary` centers the window on the first non-primary display;
  without it, Windows chooses the default location.
- `BOTW_CONTROLLER=gamepad` selects the GamePad input route. The default is
  Pro Controller; one host button press no longer activates both devices.
- `BOTW_TEST_START_SAMPLE=N` injects a bounded A press at input sample N for
  integration testing only. `BOTW_TEST_START_PRO=1` sends it through KPAD.
  Leave these unset for ordinary interactive play.
- `BOTW_CAPTURE_DIR` opts into shader/buffer/frame captures in an existing
  diagnostic directory (for example `build/logs`), including GPU frame readback.
  The latest drawn frame is refreshed every 120 swaps, including simple menus.
  Without it, captures do not add files beside the launcher.
- `BOTW_LATTE_TRACE=1` reports shader-executor rejection locations; `2` traces
  individual ALU groups. Use only for short offline replays, not normal play.
- `BOTW_WIIU_MLC_ROOT` points at a Wii U `mlc01` dump for system resources.

Diagnostic logs are retained under `build/logs`. See
`boot_sync_layout_fixed_20260910.log` for the 24,000,000-block boot trace.
The Windows-only `build/tools/botw_guest_sample.exe PID exact-executable-path`
can sample an owned diagnostic run without restarting it: it briefly suspends
and immediately resumes the busiest native thread to collect registers, then
reads guest CPU/stack state without writing guest memory. Memory can change
between those reads, so repeated samples are diagnostic rather than atomic
snapshots. `botw_memory_layout_test --benchmark` compares the checked and
direct guest-read paths in isolation; it is not an end-to-end boot benchmark.

`botw_latte_execute_test capture-directory` additionally executes captured
vertex variants 33–36. `botw_postprocess_capture_test capture-directory`
loads their VS/PS/fetch descriptors/programs, relocates guest pointers, supplies
controlled uniforms/textures/vertices, and verifies GPU pixels through the
GX2 API. Run with `build/bin` on PATH and `BOTW_HEADLESS=1`, `BOTW_MUTE=1`.
These optional tests require the local `render_fastboot_0923` capture files;
no game shader binaries are added to the source or launcher package.

`botw_latte_effect_capture_test capture-directory capture-log variant` replays
effect vertex inputs, uniform blocks and shaders for variants 25–30, then
draws through GX2 using controlled white textures. Variant 29 exercises all
132 captured vertices and its full 576-index draw. The log supplies captured
attribute strides/register uniforms. Large captured primitives (27 and 30)
must change GPU pixels; the other initial captures are sub-pixel at the test's
64x64 resolution. `botw_postprocess_test` separately checks all five effect
pixel-material formulas using nonwhite textures and known expected colors.

## Legal note

No Nintendo game content should be committed or redistributed. Users must
provide their own legally obtained title dump. The generated C is tied to that
binary and should be treated with the same care as the original executable.

The project-local RPX runtime loader is derived from DolRecomp and retains its
GPL-3.0-or-later notice. The GX2 surface-layout adapter retains its MPL-2.0
notice from Cemu. Corresponding license texts are included in this directory.
